WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.505 --> 00:00:09.467
An old saying goes: "One man's meat is another man's poison."

2
00:00:10.288 --> 00:00:15.370
In much the same way, nuclear technology brings to mind different things for different people.

3
00:00:16.130 --> 00:00:20.592
To some, it is a useful tool in medicine and an efficient source of energy.

4
00:00:21.352 --> 00:00:25.694
To others, it conjures up images of mushroom clouds and mass destruction.

5
00:00:26.574 --> 00:00:30.796
Understanding nuclear technology begins with the nucleus, the heart of the atom.

6
00:00:31.760 --> 00:00:38.685
All matter is made up of atoms, and every atom consists of particles called neutrons, protons, and electrons.

7
00:00:39.706 --> 00:00:46.370
Neutrons and protons form the solid nucleus of the atom, while the much smaller electrons orbit the nucleus

8
00:00:46.430 --> 00:00:47.951
like the planets around the sun.

9
00:00:48.832 --> 00:00:53.855
The number of these three particles in an atom determines what kind of matter, or element, it is.

10
00:00:54.736 --> 00:01:00.120
For instance, carbon has six protons, six neutrons, and six electrons.

11
00:01:01.232 --> 00:01:05.596
The ratio of these particles within an atom determines whether the atom is stable.

12
00:01:06.437 --> 00:01:13.923
Nature does not like instability, so unstable atoms undergo a natural process called "radioactive decay," in which they

13
00:01:13.983 --> 00:01:15.304
release extra particles.

14
00:01:16.205 --> 00:01:22.050
Radioactive decay can change an atom from one element into another element or into another form of the

15
00:01:22.090 --> 00:01:24.732
same element, which is known as an isotope.

16
00:01:25.573 --> 00:01:29.096
Isotopes occur when the number of neutrons present in the nucleus differs.

17
00:01:30.132 --> 00:01:35.274
Carbon-14, for example, is an isotope of carbon with eight neutrons instead of six.

18
00:01:36.155 --> 00:01:41.337
The particles released by radioactive decay can be harmful, but they can be put to good use as

19
00:01:41.397 --> 00:01:46.379
well. In the field of nuclear medicine, radiation is used to treat cancer.

20
00:01:47.099 --> 00:01:52.902
Radioactive elements can also be injected into the body and tracked with imaging equipment to provide a look

21
00:01:53.002 --> 00:01:54.742
inside the body without surgery.

22
00:01:55.543 --> 00:01:58.444
This procedure is very useful in medical diagnosis.

23
00:01:59.404 --> 00:02:05.248
When the United States' Food and Drug Administration tests new drugs for safety, many of the tests are

24
00:02:05.288 --> 00:02:07.270
conducted using nuclear processes.

25
00:02:08.030 --> 00:02:11.833
Carbon dating is another technique that relies on nuclear processes.

26
00:02:12.653 --> 00:02:17.757
In the field of archaeology, it is used to determine the age of artifacts based on the radioactive

27
00:02:17.797 --> 00:02:19.218
decay of carbon-14.

28
00:02:19.938 --> 00:02:27.123
Because the carbon-14 isotope decays into carbon-12 at a very slow and constant rate, you can determine the

29
00:02:27.223 --> 00:02:31.045
age of an object by measuring the amount of carbon-14 it contains.

30
00:02:32.065 --> 00:02:36.146
Museums also use carbon dating to verify the age of pieces of art.

31
00:02:36.926 --> 00:02:40.027
Nuclear technology is present even in our everyday lives.

32
00:02:41.008 --> 00:02:46.069
Many smoke detectors, for example, employ a radioactive material to trigger the alarm.

33
00:02:47.209 --> 00:02:51.651
In addition to releasing particles, radioactive decay also releases energy.

34
00:02:52.431 --> 00:02:59.217
Uranium, a much heavier radioactive element than carbon-14, is the one most often used to produce nuclear power.

35
00:02:59.998 --> 00:03:07.521
One of its isotopes, uranium-235 (U-235), can be induced to split in a process called nuclear fission.

36
00:03:08.281 --> 00:03:14.924
If a free neutron hits a U-235 nucleus, the atom will absorb the neutron but will become unstable.

37
00:03:15.665 --> 00:03:18.006
It will then split in order to stabilize itself.

38
00:03:18.786 --> 00:03:27.034
Each split releases several neutrons, which cause more fission reactions, in turn releasing more neutrons—igniting a chain reaction.

39
00:03:27.854 --> 00:03:30.195
This process releases great amounts of energy.

40
00:03:30.215 --> 00:03:34.237
Nuclear power plants use this energy to produce electricity.

41
00:03:34.997 --> 00:03:40.680
The energy of the chain reaction is used to create steam to turn a turbine, just as in

42
00:03:40.860 --> 00:03:42.021
other power stations.

43
00:03:43.181 --> 00:03:48.924
One pound of uranium, however, is able to produce 3.7 million times as much energy as a pound

44
00:03:48.964 --> 00:03:49.324
of coal.

45
00:03:50.072 --> 00:03:57.558
Plutonium, an element that is formed in uranium-based nuclear reactions, can also be used for fission, making nuclear

46
00:03:57.638 --> 00:03:58.859
energy very efficient.

47
00:03:59.720 --> 00:04:05.425
Nuclear power accounts for 17 percent of the world's electricity, and many people see it as a viable

48
00:04:05.505 --> 00:04:07.106
alternative to fossil fuels.

49
00:04:07.907 --> 00:04:13.031
Our earth has only a limited amount of coal and oil left, but uranium is abundant.

50
00:04:13.851 --> 00:04:19.196
In addition, a well-designed and maintained nuclear power plant produces clean electricity.

51
00:04:20.008 --> 00:04:27.472
In fact, a coal-fired plant may disperse more radioactive substances into the atmosphere than a properly functioning nuclear

52
00:04:27.492 --> 00:04:35.815
facility. Plants that burn fossil fuels also eject tons of other pollutants, such as carbon dioxide, sulfur, and

53
00:04:35.976 --> 00:04:37.916
ash, into the atmosphere each year.

54
00:04:38.717 --> 00:04:41.198
Nuclear stations do not release these pollutants.

55
00:04:42.338 --> 00:04:45.300
There are, however, disadvantages to nuclear power.

56
00:04:46.032 --> 00:04:52.637
Exposure to radioactive particles can cause radiation sickness and lead to cancer or other health problems.

57
00:04:53.477 --> 00:04:57.160
Many precautions are taken to ensure that nuclear reactors are safe.

58
00:04:57.861 --> 00:05:05.606
Since particles released by radioactive decay cannot penetrate thick concrete, reactors are encased in concrete buildings that act

59
00:05:05.686 --> 00:05:06.186
as shields.

60
00:05:07.147 --> 00:05:13.752
However, despite the numerous safety measures taken at nuclear power plants, history has shown that accidents happen.

61
00:05:14.632 --> 00:05:22.256
In 1979, radiation leaked from the Three-Mile Island Nuclear Generating Station in the U.S., and thousands of people

62
00:05:22.296 --> 00:05:23.377
had to be evacuated.

63
00:05:24.177 --> 00:05:25.298
Luckily, no one died.

64
00:05:25.918 --> 00:05:33.802
In 1986, an accidental explosion at the Chernobyl Nuclear Power Station in the former Soviet Union was not

65
00:05:33.842 --> 00:05:40.106
contained, and thousands of civilians and military personnel were exposed to radiation.

66
00:05:41.126 --> 00:05:48.566
Thirty people died from the explosion or soon after, and within the next several years, thousands more experienced

67
00:05:48.646 --> 00:05:52.628
a variety of health problems due to the radiation they received.

68
00:05:53.369 --> 00:05:59.472
In recent years, many people have also become concerned that nuclear reactors are vulnerable to attack.

69
00:06:00.352 --> 00:06:07.056
A bomb or sabotage at a nuclear facility could cause a horrendous disaster, as the accident at Chernobyl

70
00:06:07.076 --> 00:06:07.576
has shown.

71
00:06:08.557 --> 00:06:15.737
Not surprisingly, the risks associated with nuclear power have given rise to the expression "NIMBY," an acronym for

72
00:06:15.757 --> 00:06:17.318
"not in my backyard."

73
00:06:18.139 --> 00:06:20.961
This phrase captures the sentiments of much of the public.

74
00:06:21.741 --> 00:06:28.287
Although people appreciate the electricity a nuclear reactor provides, they do not want a potential radioactive "bomb" near

75
00:06:28.327 --> 00:06:28.867
their homes.

76
00:06:29.908 --> 00:06:34.111
The NIMBY sentiment applies even more to the disposal of nuclear waste.

77
00:06:34.812 --> 00:06:40.236
Waste products from nuclear reactors are highly radioactive and cannot be simply thrown away.

78
00:06:41.168 --> 00:06:48.111
Even though containers for nuclear waste are designed to stop radioactive leaks, the material inside can remain toxic

79
00:06:48.131 --> 00:06:49.311
for thousands of years.

80
00:06:50.012 --> 00:06:54.514
Even the strongest containers will deteriorate and fail before the material is safe.

81
00:06:55.434 --> 00:07:01.676
Used fuel rods from nuclear reactors are cooled in tightly sealed containers in pools of water for several

82
00:07:01.736 --> 00:07:04.137
years and then buried deep underground.

83
00:07:04.838 --> 00:07:10.300
There is no guarantee, however, that measures like these will be enough to protect people and the environment.

84
00:07:11.524 --> 00:07:17.666
Nuclear technology poses another less direct, but equally important risk—nuclear proliferation.

85
00:07:18.386 --> 00:07:25.348
Although nuclear weapons and nuclear power plants require different grades of uranium, the technology used to produce nuclear

86
00:07:25.388 --> 00:07:30.189
power can also be used to develop weapons capable of killing millions of people.

87
00:07:31.090 --> 00:07:33.910
The two superpowers of the cold war, the U.S.

88
00:07:33.970 --> 00:07:39.232
and Russia, have enough nuclear bombs to kill every person on earth many times over.

89
00:07:40.052 --> 00:07:47.417
As more nations acquire nuclear power and possibly nuclear weapons, the likelihood of a devastating nuclear war increases.

90
00:07:48.258 --> 00:07:54.302
Clearly, nuclear technology has advantages and disadvantages, and there is a heated debate about its use.

91
00:07:55.163 --> 00:08:00.466
As the countries of the world plan for a cleaner, safer, and more energy-efficient future, they must weigh

92
00:08:00.486 --> 00:08:02.908
the pros and cons of this technology carefully.
